WEBVTT

00:00:07.388 --> 00:00:12.468 
In the previous tutorial, we explored the basics of WoT forms and bindings.

00:00:12.468 --> 00:00:19.730 
 Now that we understand their structure, let's see how protocol bindings work in practice.

00:00:19.730 --> 00:00:25.671 
 When you want to write a Consumer application, you don't need any prior knowledge about the protocol being used.

00:00:25.671 --> 00:00:33.393 
Instead, the Consumer reads the Thing Description, looks at the available forms, and selects one it understands.

00:00:33.393 --> 00:00:38.383 
 Let's walk through four protocols using our smart coffee machine example.

00:00:38.383 --> 00:00:44.112 
For each one, we'll trace exactly how a WoT operation relates to real network messages.

00:00:44.112 --> 00:00:53.900 
 First, we want the Consumer to perform the " readproperty" operation on "coffeeBeansLeft" so we can get the remaining

00:00:53.900 --> 00:00:59.511 
 To achieve this, the Consumer reads the form and translates it into a network request.

00:00:59.511 --> 00:01:04.350 
The " href" tells it the address and that HTTP is the protocol.

00:01:04.350 --> 00:01:11.575 
Then the "htv methodName" field says to use a " GET" request and the "contentType" tells it to expect a

00:01:11.575 --> 00:01:12.916 
JSON in return.

00:01:12.916 --> 00:01:15.695 
So the Consumer sends the following request:

00:01:15.695 --> 00:01:21.074 
 The Thing receives this, reads its internal sensor, and responds:

00:01:21.074 --> 00:01:29.627 
 The Consumer parses the JSON body - a single number, exactly as the coffeeBeansLeft data schema declared - and

00:01:29.627 --> 00:01:36.365 
now has the value of 320 grams. The form told it how to fetch; the data schema told it what

00:01:36.365 --> 00:01:37.645 
shape to expect.

00:01:37.645 --> 00:01:42.459 
 Now the Consumer wants to invoke the "brewCoffee" action.

00:01:42.459 --> 00:01:48.840 
 The Consumer reads this form just like before — but now " href" starts with " coap", so it

00:01:48.840 --> 00:01:51.592 
knows to use CoAP instead of HTTP.

00:01:51.592 --> 00:01:54.558 
The "cov method" field says "POST".

00:01:54.558 --> 00:02:01.988 
The contentType is "application cbor", so this time the Consumer encodes the action input for a medium sized coffee in

00:02:01.988 --> 00:02:02.929 
CBOR.

00:02:02.929 --> 00:02:06.485 
 The resulting CoAP message looks something like this:

00:02:06.485 --> 00:02:14.790 
 The Thing receives the message and sends back a CoAP response with code "2.04 Changed", confirming the action was

00:02:14.790 --> 00:02:15.609 
accepted.

00:02:15.609 --> 00:02:23.525 
From the Consumer's perspective, this was identical to invoking the HTTP action — it performed an " invokeaction" operation and

00:02:23.525 --> 00:02:25.151 
got a success response.

00:02:25.151 --> 00:02:33.228 
 The fact that it used CoAP instead of HTTP, or CBOR instead of JSON, was determined entirely by what

00:02:33.228 --> 00:02:34.556 
was written in the form.

00:02:34.556 --> 00:02:39.105 
 For the "lowOnWater" event, the form looks like this:

00:02:39.105 --> 00:02:45.529 
The " op" field is " subscribeevent", which tells the Consumer it needs to listen rather than request.

00:02:45.529 --> 00:02:53.444 
The href points to the MQTT broker, and the `mqv filter` term gives the topic 
filter - lowOnWater - that

00:02:53.444 --> 00:02:55.556 
the Consumer should subscribe to.

00:02:55.556 --> 00:03:04.917 
The mqv:qos term tells the Consumer that the Thing publishes these events at QoS level 1, MQTT's at-least-once delivery.

00:03:04.917 --> 00:03:10.060 
 So the Consumer connects to the broker and sends:

00:03:10.060 --> 00:03:16.148 
On the Thing side, when the water level drops below the threshold, it publishes to the same topic:

00:03:16.148 --> 00:03:19.467 
 The broker forwards this to the Consumer.

00:03:19.467 --> 00:03:27.825 
 The Consumer receives the message, parses the text as declared by "contentType", and now has the event data: 50

00:03:27.825 --> 00:03:29.262 
milliliters remaining.

00:03:29.262 --> 00:03:37.966 
What's important here is that the WoT operation " subscribeevent" naturally maps to MQTT's publish subscribe model. The Consumer didn't

00:03:37.966 --> 00:03:53.377 
need to know it was MQTT — it simply performed a " subscribeevent" operation, and the form told it exactly

00:03:53.377 --> 00:04:00.818 
 We've now seen three protocols applied to different affordances of our coffee machine. Let's see how we'd adapt it

00:04:00.818 --> 00:04:04.437 
to a completely different kind of protocol: Modbus.

00:04:04.437 --> 00:04:10.748 
 Modbus is a traditional industrial protocol widely used in automation and control systems.

00:04:10.748 --> 00:04:19.193 
While it predates modern web technologies, WoT protocol bindings make it possible to expose Modbus-based devices through a standardized Thing

00:04:19.193 --> 00:04:20.056 
Description.

00:04:20.056 --> 00:04:27.848 
This allows legacy industrial equipment to be integrated into modern web-based systems, without changing the underlying protocol.

00:04:27.848 --> 00:04:33.176 
 Let's trace how the Consumer translates this form into a Modbus message.

00:04:33.176 --> 00:04:37.804 
The "base" URI gives it the device address and port 502.

00:04:37.804 --> 00:04:43.320 
The number 1 at the end is the Unit ID — the Modbus device identifier on the bus.

00:04:43.320 --> 00:04:47.862 
The " href" value "40003" is the register address.

00:04:47.862 --> 00:05:11.445 
The " modv:function" field says "readHoldingRegisters", identifying the type of register being addressed - Holding Registers can be both read

00:05:11.445 --> 00:05:18.659 
The "contentType" is set to "application octet-stream", which means the expected response is a sequence of bytes.

00:05:18.659 --> 00:05:22.270 
Putting this all together, the Consumer sends its request.

00:05:22.270 --> 00:05:30.952 
 The Consumer receives those two bytes and interprets them as a 16-bit 
big-endian integer. Because the Thing Description specifies

00:05:30.952 --> 00:05:39.184 
that 
"waterLevel" is an integer, the WoT runtime automatically turns this into the number 300— the water level in milliliters.

00:05:39.184 --> 00:05:42.921 
 This is WoT's value fully on display.

00:05:42.921 --> 00:05:56.625 
A developer writing a Consumer application only needs to work with " readproperty" operations and Thing Descriptions - the protocol

00:05:56.625 --> 00:06:03.480 
binding underneath handles the Modbus function codes, register addresses, and byte encoding on their behalf.

00:06:03.480 --> 00:06:10.580 
All of that knowledge lives in the form, expressed through "modv function", " href", and the base URI.

00:06:10.580 --> 00:06:15.385 
The form is the bridge between the abstracted WoT operations and the protocol.

00:06:17.219 --> 00:06:18.581 
To summarize:

00:06:18.581 --> 00:06:22.682 
- Bindings define how interactions are executed over the network

00:06:22.682 --> 00:06:26.335 
- They are expressed using "forms" in the Thing Description

00:06:26.335 --> 00:06:29.764 
- Different protocols fit different interaction patterns

00:06:29.764 --> 00:06:34.100 
- WoT unifies them under a single, consistent interaction model

00:06:34.100 --> 00:06:40.003 
